            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Leonidas</strong><br>\nLeonidas I was a king of the Greek city-state of Sparta from about 490 B.C. until his death at the Battle of Thermopylae in 480 B.C. He was a member of the Agiad dynasty, which claimed descent from the hero Heracles. Born around 540 B.C., Leonidas was the third son of King Anaxandrides II, and it was not expected that he would ascend to the throne.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nLeonidas became king after the death of his half-brother Cleomenes I, who left no male heir. Cleomenes's only surviving child was a daughter, Gorgo, who Leonidas married. This marriage secured his claim to the throne and strengthened his position as king. Leonidas was chosen because he was a proven warrior and had shown qualities of leadership.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Training</strong><br>\nLike all Spartan males, Leonidas underwent the rigorous military training mandated by the Spartan agoge system. He was trained to endure hardships, excel in physical combat, and survive on minimal resources. The agoge emphasized discipline, obedience, and excellence in martial skills, preparing Leonidas for his future role as a military leader.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
