      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Spartan Society</strong><br>\nSparta was unique in ancient Greece for its social system and constitution, which were entirely focused on military training and excellence. As a king, Leonidas's role was to maintain the military prowess of Sparta. He was one of the two Spartan kings who jointly ruled, with one overseeing military affairs while the other took care of domestic issues.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Battle of Thermopylae</strong><br>\nThe Battle of Thermopylae is the most famous battle of Leonidas's reign. In 480 B.C., a Persian army under King Xerxes I invaded Greece. Leonidas led a small Greek force to defend the pass of Thermopylae, a strategic point on the invasion route. Despite being vastly outnumbered, Leonidas and his men held the pass for three days.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The \"300 Spartans\"</strong><br>\nLeonidas is perhaps best known for the stand of his \"300 Spartans\" at the Battle of Thermopylae. Although the force with him included soldiers from other Greek states, it was the Spartans' valor and discipline that became legendary. Their stand has been celebrated as an example of heroic resistance against overwhelming odds.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
